&lt;h2&gt;


  How I Built It
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Qwen3 8B, an open-weight model running locally through Ollama, is the creative engine.&lt;/strong&gt; Every generated script comes from the model; the app does not substitute a canned script when inference fails.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The application uses a Node.js HTTP server and a plain HTML, CSS, and JavaScript frontend. It has no npm runtime dependencies. The backend fetches stories from free public API endpoints and caches each niche's feed for five minutes:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://hn.algolia.com/api" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Hacker News search through Algolia&lt;/a&gt; supplies recent AI, technology, and startup stories with points and comment counts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://developers.forem.com/api/v1" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The DEV API&lt;/a&gt; supplies coding and design posts with descriptions and community reactions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://www.spaceflightnewsapi.net/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Spaceflight News API&lt;/a&gt; supplies recent space reporting with publisher summaries.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These signals need context: community activity is not proof that something is trending on Instagram, and recent space reporting is not a popularity ranking. Reelflow labels the distinction instead of inventing a viral score.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After a user selects a story, the server sends its available source context, desired duration, and delivery style to Ollama's &lt;code&gt;/api/chat&lt;/code&gt; endpoint. A JSON schema defines the four required scenes and their voiceover, visual, and overlay fields. The server validates the response again before displaying it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The prompt tells the model to treat source text as data, use only supplied facts, and avoid invented statistics or quotes. When a source supplies only a headline, the interface discloses that limitation and the prompt requests an exploratory angle. These safeguards do not guarantee factual accuracy: the creator still needs to check the original source before recording.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I used Codex to help implement and test the application. The generated Reel content itself is powered by the local Qwen model.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why Does Open Innovation Matter?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open innovation makes the economics and ownership of this tool more flexible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Experimenting does not incur a paid inference API charge for each draft.&lt;/strong&gt; A creator can change the tone or regenerate an outline using their own machine. Hardware and electricity still cost money, and running a hosted service would add costs, but there is no per-generation model-provider bill in the local setup.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The writing process stays on the machine running Reelflow.&lt;/strong&gt; With the default local configuration, the selected story context and generated script are processed by Ollama on that computer. Discovering fresh stories still requires internet access, so this is not a fully offline research tool.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The creative engine can change with the product.&lt;/strong&gt; The model is configurable through &lt;code&gt;OLLAMA_MODEL&lt;/code&gt;, and the prompt and output schema are directly editable. I can adapt the workflow to a specific creator's niche or try another compatible local model without rebuilding around a different paid API contract. This version uses the existing Qwen3 model; I have not fine-tuned it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A closed API could also generate a script. The difference here is that I can run and inspect the application around a model I control locally, choose when to change it, and keep inference independent of a hosted provider's pricing. For a small tool aimed at repeat creative work, those choices matter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sometimes I need to see a problem before I can explain it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Who calls this service? What happens if the request fails? Is this actually one feature, or three separate problems hiding under the same name?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A few boxes and arrows can make those questions easier to answer. So can a mind map that starts messy and gradually becomes a plan.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's why I still like UML—and why I rebuilt &lt;strong&gt;Codiva PlantUML&lt;/strong&gt;, my browser-based editor for text-based diagrams.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes, UML. The thing you may remember from university assignments, enormous class diagrams, and arguments about which arrowhead to use.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I think there's still a lot to like once you bring it back to everyday development.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://portafolio.codiva.cl/codiva-plantuml/" class="crayons-btn crayons-btn--primary" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Open Codiva PlantUML&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A little history: why all those boxes existed
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before UML, there were several competing approaches to modeling object-oriented systems. Grady Booch, James Rumbaugh, and Ivar Jacobson brought their methods together, helping create the &lt;strong&gt;Unified Modeling Language&lt;/strong&gt;. It became an OMG standard in 1997.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The idea was useful: give people a shared visual vocabulary for describing software. A relationship or interaction shouldn't need an entirely new notation every time you change teams.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then came the baggage many developers associate with it: elaborate modeling tools, diagrams produced as deliverables, and designs that took longer to maintain than the code they described.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If your introduction to UML involved modeling every getter and setter before writing a working program, I understand the lack of enthusiasm.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But I still find value in the smaller habit underneath it: &lt;strong&gt;put a model of the problem somewhere you can inspect it.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A sequence diagram can reveal a round trip nobody noticed. A state diagram can expose a transition nobody defined. Even an unfinished sketch can show that two people have been imagining different systems throughout the same meeting.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those are useful discoveries before implementation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A diagram can help you think before it helps you document
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We often talk about diagrams as something you make after understanding a system, so someone else can understand it too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I like the earlier stage just as much: making a diagram &lt;em&gt;to find out what I understand&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Say you're planning an asynchronous report feature. In prose, it sounds simple:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The user requests a report, a worker generates it, and we notify them when it's ready.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start drawing it, and the questions appear:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Who owns the report's status?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What happens when the worker retries?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Can the user request the same report twice?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What does “ready” mean if notification fails?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The picture gives those questions somewhere to live. You can change the model while the change is still cheap.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The diagram doesn't have to represent the entire system. Often the most useful one answers a single question.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Mind maps are a great place to start
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before I know the sequence of events, I may only know the pieces of the problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's where mind maps help: take a vague feature, give it a few branches, and keep splitting until the unknowns become visible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;PlantUML supports mind maps too.&lt;/strong&gt; They aren't part of the UML standard; PlantUML extends beyond UML into other useful diagram types.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's a small example you can paste into the editor:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;@startmindmap
* Asynchronous reports
** User experience
*** Request a report
*** Check progress
*** Download the result
** Processing
*** Queue the job
*** Generate the file
*** Retry failures
** Questions
*** Duplicate requests?
*** File expiration?
*** Notification failure?
@endmindmap
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The asterisks define the hierarchy. Add another level when an idea needs breaking down; move a branch when you realize it belongs elsewhere.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I find this useful for feature planning, exploring an unfamiliar domain, or organizing the questions I want to take into a technical discussion.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An outline works too. Seeing its branches together can make gaps and uneven complexity easier to notice.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then, when one branch needs a closer look, you can turn it into a more specific diagram.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A few UML diagrams still earn their place
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You don't need to learn the whole notation to get something useful from it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;When I want to understand…&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;I reach for…&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Who talks to whom, and in what order&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A sequence diagram&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Which states exist and what changes them&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A state diagram&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;How a workflow branches&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;An activity diagram&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;How domain concepts relate&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A class diagram&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;How the system's parts connect&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A component diagram&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A class diagram, for example, can show the relationships between an order, its items, and a payment without listing every method in the codebase.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A sequence diagram can make the difference between “request accepted” and “work completed” explicit:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;@startuml
actor User
participant API
queue Jobs
participant Worker

User -&amp;gt; API: Request report
API -&amp;gt; Jobs: Enqueue job
API --&amp;gt; User: Accepted + report ID
Jobs -&amp;gt; Worker: Deliver job
Worker -&amp;gt; Worker: Generate report

alt generation succeeds
  Worker -&amp;gt; API: Mark report ready
else generation fails
  Worker -&amp;gt; API: Record failure
end
@enduml
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This sketch also leaves things unresolved. How does the user learn that the report is ready? What happens after a failure?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's a useful next conversation. The diagram has already helped identify it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why I like writing diagrams as text
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Visual editors are great for sketching freely. With PlantUML, I like being able to describe the relationships and let the renderer handle their arrangement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That gives me a few practical advantages:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Reviewable changes.&lt;/strong&gt; A pull request can show a new participant, message, or state transition.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Source beside the code.&lt;/strong&gt; A &lt;code&gt;.puml&lt;/code&gt; file can follow the same branches and review process as the feature it explains.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Easy reuse.&lt;/strong&gt; Copy a flow, adjust a few lines, and explore an alternative.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Less time arranging boxes.&lt;/strong&gt; I can focus on the interaction before worrying about presentation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are trade-offs. Automatic layout won't always look the way you want, and a large diagram can become difficult to read regardless of how you generate it. Text also doesn't keep documentation accurate by itself; someone still has to maintain it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For me, the sweet spot is a small, editable diagram with a clear purpose.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you already use Mermaid and it fits your workflow, great. I see the habit of keeping useful diagrams as text as the bigger win. PlantUML happens to be the tool I wanted to build around.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Meet Codiva PlantUML
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I had an older editor built with CodeMirror 5, Bootstrap, and CDN dependencies. It rendered through the public PlantUML server and had been sitting there for a while.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I rebuilt it with &lt;strong&gt;Astro, React islands, CodeMirror 6, and my own containerized PlantUML engine&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The goal was a convenient place to open an example, work through an idea, and export a diagram without setting up a local environment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The editor includes:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Syntax highlighting and autocomplete for participants you've declared.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Syntax diagnostics with line highlighting.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Formatting from the context menu.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Dark mode.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;SVG and PNG export.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Six examples to start from.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No account required.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Rendering happens on my backend, so diagram source is sent to that server when you use the hosted editor.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's free to use. There's an optional coffee link in the footer to help with the server bill.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A couple of things I learned rebuilding it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Running the engine myself gave me more control over responsiveness and diagnostics. It also gave me a few bugs worth remembering.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Keep the JVM warm
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Launching a new JVM for each render took around &lt;strong&gt;1,115 ms&lt;/strong&gt; in my setup. Keeping PlantUML running brought the warm local engine measurement down to around &lt;strong&gt;14 ms&lt;/strong&gt;, averaged over 10 requests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Over HTTPS, I observed roughly &lt;strong&gt;180 ms&lt;/strong&gt;, compared with around &lt;strong&gt;560 ms&lt;/strong&gt; for the public endpoint in my comparison. Those are observations from my deployment, rather than a general benchmark; network location, diagram complexity, and server load all matter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;PlantUML's built-in &lt;a href="https://plantuml.com/picoweb" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;PicoWeb server&lt;/a&gt; provides a simple way to keep the engine running:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;java &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-jar&lt;/span&gt; plantuml.jar &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-picoweb&lt;/span&gt;:8777
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  A successful response can contain an unsuccessful diagram
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My sequence-diagram test passed. A class diagram then failed because the container was missing Graphviz.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The surprising part: the response was &lt;strong&gt;HTTP 200 with a valid SVG containing the error message&lt;/strong&gt;. My status-code check was happy. The person trying to render the diagram wasn't.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I used &lt;strong&gt;Smetana&lt;/strong&gt;, PlantUML's Java port of Graphviz, to handle layout in my setup. The editor also needed useful diagnostics rather than leaving users to read an error drawn inside an image.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's the improvement I care about most: if you make a syntax mistake while thinking through a flow, the editor should help you get back to the flow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
  Implementation detail: selecting Smetana and mapping error lines
  &lt;p&gt;PlantUML supports selecting Smetana in diagram source:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;!pragma layout smetana
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Its documentation also lists the CLI option &lt;code&gt;-Playout=smetana&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In my server integration, the proxy injects the pragma into the rendering copy, leaving the editor contents and exported source as written. That added line means diagnostic positions need mapping back to the original source.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My application layer uses PlantUML diagnostics to return a 422 response with a line number for syntax errors. That response contract is part of the editor's backend; starting PicoWeb alone doesn't provide it.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;br&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Give one of your ideas a diagram
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you've been carrying a feature around in your head, try putting its main branches into a mind map. If a flow keeps getting lost in paragraphs, try a sequence diagram.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's the kind of everyday use I built this editor for.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://portafolio.codiva.cl/codiva-plantuml/" class="crayons-btn crayons-btn--primary" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Try Codiva PlantUML — no account needed&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'd love feedback on the editor, especially anything that gets in the way of turning an idea into a readable diagram.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Do you still use UML—or did diagrams find their way back into your workflow through mind maps, Mermaid, or something else?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  References and further reading
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.omg.org/uml/why-uml-is-important.htm" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;OMG: UML's origins and purpose&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://plantuml.com/mindmap-diagram" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;PlantUML: mind map syntax&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://plantuml.com/layout-engines" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;PlantUML: layout engines&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://plantuml.com/picoweb" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;PlantUML: PicoWeb server&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every .NET codebase I have worked on hits the same wall sooner or later. The test suite grows, CI goes from three minutes to twenty, and every pull request pays the full price, even a one-line rename deep inside a helper class.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Splitting the suite or buying bigger runners helps for a while. But most of those tests could never have noticed the change in the first place. They run anyway because nothing tells CI which ones matter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So I built &lt;strong&gt;TestDetta&lt;/strong&gt;: a .NET tool and GitHub Action that reads your diff and runs only the test classes that can observe it. It follows one rule throughout: &lt;strong&gt;when it cannot be sure, it runs more tests, never fewer.&lt;/strong&gt; A missed test is a bug. An extra test only costs CI time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This post covers how it works, how to try it in a few minutes, and how to check that it holds up on your own code before you trust it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A real example
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is a real change to &lt;a href="https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-dotnet" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;OpenTelemetry .NET&lt;/a&gt;: a local variable renamed inside one method. It changes no behaviour, but a normal pipeline still runs all 30 test projects and 274 test classes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TestDetta works it out like this:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Projects:&lt;/strong&gt; 2 of 30 test projects are affected. The other 28 are not even built.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Symbols:&lt;/strong&gt; 1 member changed, &lt;code&gt;WarnIfConfigFileEnvVarDiffers&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Coverage map:&lt;/strong&gt; 8 test classes ran that method on &lt;code&gt;main&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Result:&lt;/strong&gt; 8 of 274 classes run, 266 are skipped.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those 8 classes are exactly the ones that fail when that method is broken. Recorded test time went from &lt;strong&gt;5 m 59 s to 22.4 s&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Try it in two minutes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TestDetta is a .NET tool (it runs on the .NET 10 SDK; your projects can target older frameworks):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;dotnet tool &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--global&lt;/span&gt; TestDetta
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Ask what your branch affects, and why:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;td affected &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--base&lt;/span&gt; origin/main
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;See the &lt;code&gt;dotnet test&lt;/code&gt; commands it would run, without running anything:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;td &lt;span class="nb"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--base&lt;/span&gt; origin/main &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--dry-run&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;And run only the affected tests:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;td &lt;span class="nb"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--base&lt;/span&gt; origin/main
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The output looks like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;$ td test --base origin/main
TestDetta 0.2.2 · 1 changed file · 2 affected test projects
Tests to run
┌──────────────────┬──────────┬─────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Test project     │ Run      │ Classes │ Why                                                      │
├──────────────────┼──────────┼─────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ tests/Shop.Tests │ filtered │  3 / 41 │ 3 of 41 test class(es) run or reference the changed code │
│ tests/Api.Tests  │ skipped  │  0 / 12 │ no test class runs or references the changed code        │
└──────────────────┴──────────┴─────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  tests/Shop.Tests → 3 classes
    Shop.Tests.CartTests
    Shop.Tests.CheckoutTests
    Shop.Tests.OrderTests
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Without a coverage map, TestDetta decides by tracing names through your source. For sharper selection, record a map once on your default branch:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;td record
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This runs every test with coverage and writes one map per test project to &lt;code&gt;.testdetta/coverage&lt;/code&gt; (add &lt;code&gt;.testdetta/&lt;/code&gt; to your &lt;code&gt;.gitignore&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Need machine-readable output? &lt;code&gt;td affected --format json&lt;/code&gt; gives you the selection for your own scripts, &lt;code&gt;--format paths&lt;/code&gt; lists the test projects, and &lt;code&gt;--format commands&lt;/code&gt; prints the &lt;code&gt;dotnet test&lt;/code&gt; commands.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How it decides
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each step narrows the selection only when it has evidence. Otherwise it falls back to something wider.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Projects.&lt;/strong&gt; Changed files are mapped to their projects and followed through &lt;code&gt;ProjectReference&lt;/code&gt;s. Build-wide files such as &lt;code&gt;Directory.Build.props&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;global.json&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;.editorconfig&lt;/code&gt; affect every project below them.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Symbols.&lt;/strong&gt; For C#, Visual Basic and F# files, the old and new syntax trees are compared (Roslyn for C# and VB). Comment and whitespace edits select nothing. Otherwise TestDetta knows exactly which types and members changed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Coverage.&lt;/strong&gt; A map of which test class ran which method, recorded at the base commit, gives the test classes for changed methods, properties and constructors directly.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Names.&lt;/strong&gt; What coverage cannot see, such as new code, fields and constants, is traced through the source by name until it reaches test classes or code the map knows.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Fallbacks.&lt;/strong&gt; When a change cannot be traced, the whole test project runs. That covers non-code files the build uses (&lt;code&gt;appsettings.json&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;.csproj&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;.resx&lt;/code&gt;), global usings, assembly attributes, and types a framework discovers through reflection (controllers, message handlers, EF configurations).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is one more safety net: if a filter matches no test at all, TestDetta treats that as its own bug and runs the project again without the filter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A few details took a lot of work to get right:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Maps don't have to be fresh.&lt;/strong&gt; The best map is one recorded at your branch's merge base. A map up to 50 commits older still works: TestDetta also selects the classes that ran anything changed in between that could reach your change.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Source generators are handled.&lt;/strong&gt; Generated code is read from the PDBs during recording. When a change reaches a generator in your repository, TestDetta builds the test projects that use it and compares what is generated, framework by framework.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;#if&lt;/code&gt; code is not missed.&lt;/strong&gt; C# files are parsed with each target framework's real preprocessor symbols, and with every symbol their &lt;code&gt;#if&lt;/code&gt;s name.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Your test configuration is respected.&lt;/strong&gt; Your own &lt;code&gt;--filter&lt;/code&gt;, run settings and runner arguments are combined with TestDetta's class filter, so it only ever narrows your runs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  In GitHub Actions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The usual setup records a map on every push to &lt;code&gt;main&lt;/code&gt; and runs only the affected tests on pull requests:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;on&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;push&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;branches&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;pull_request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="na"&gt;permissions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;contents&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;read&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;actions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;read&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# lets pull requests find the newest coverage map in the Actions cache&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="na"&gt;jobs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;runs-on&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;ubuntu-latest&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;steps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;uses&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;actions/checkout@v7&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# pin to a commit SHA&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="na"&gt;with&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="na"&gt;fetch-depth&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# TestDetta compares with the merge base&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="na"&gt;persist-credentials&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;

      &lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;uses&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;actions/setup-dotnet@v6&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# pin to a commit SHA&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="na"&gt;with&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="na"&gt;global-json-file&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;global.json&lt;/span&gt;

      &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# Full test run of main, which also records the coverage map&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;github.event_name == 'push'&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="na"&gt;uses&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;TestDetta/action@a2f55a8252840a367f2f7a9ebd6733de3ebfb2f5&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# v0.2.2&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="na"&gt;with&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="na"&gt;mode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;record&lt;/span&gt;

      &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# Pull requests run only what the change can affect&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;github.event_name == 'pull_request'&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="na"&gt;uses&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;TestDetta/action@a2f55a8252840a367f2f7a9ebd6733de3ebfb2f5&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# v0.2.2&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="na"&gt;with&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="na"&gt;mode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="na"&gt;dotnet-test-args&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;--logger trx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Maps live in the Actions cache, keyed by commit, and pull requests read the map of their merge base. Every run writes a job summary explaining what ran and why.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not on GitHub? GitLab CI, Azure DevOps, Jenkins and Bitbucket use the same two commands (&lt;code&gt;td record&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;td test&lt;/code&gt;) with a folder of per-commit maps on a shared disk or object storage (&lt;code&gt;--maps-dir&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Don't trust it, check it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A tool that decides which tests to skip has to earn your trust on your own codebase, not on a marketing page. TestDetta ships two ways to check it, and neither needs a license:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Shadow mode&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;td test --shadow&lt;/code&gt;, or &lt;code&gt;mode: shadow&lt;/code&gt; in the action) keeps running your full suite and compares the result with what the selection would have run. When a test fails outside the selection, it reruns that class to tell a real miss from a flaky or pre-existing failure. Run it for a couple of weeks of normal pull requests and you have evidence from your own code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Audit&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;td audit&lt;/code&gt;, or &lt;code&gt;mode: audit&lt;/code&gt;) doesn't wait for real failures. It breaks your code on purpose, one member at a time with IL mutants in a copy of the working tree, runs every test that can see each change, and reports any failing class the selection left out. It also reports the 95% upper bound of the miss rate. Your sources and build output are only read.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How it is tested
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I run the same check at scale on &lt;strong&gt;53 open-source .NET repositories&lt;/strong&gt;, including Polly, Newtonsoft.Json, AutoMapper, Serilog, Markdig, Refit, Autofac, NodaTime, FastEndpoints, FluentValidation and OpenTelemetry. Members are changed one at a time, and every test class that fails must be one TestDetta selected.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;From the latest round (2026-10-13 to 2026-10-15):&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;3,071&lt;/strong&gt; changes broke at least one test.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;5&lt;/strong&gt; misses, each a documented limitation (more on those below). Earlier real misses were fixed, each with a regression test that fails without the fix.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The median repository ran &lt;strong&gt;35%&lt;/strong&gt; of its test classes per change. In 20 of the 53 repositories it was a quarter or less, and Polly got down to 3%.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To be upfront: some repositories gain much less. Mapperly ran 97% and AutoMapper 86%, because nearly every test there goes through the same central code paths. If most of your tests touch everything, test impact analysis can't save much, and the evaluation shows that honestly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Limitations
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TestDetta is static and coverage-based analysis, not magic. Here is what it can't fully see:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Code that runs once per process&lt;/strong&gt; (static constructors, lazy singletons, EF model building) is credited to the first test class that triggers it. TestDetta traces changes to such code by name instead, but a cache you wrote by hand (a &lt;code&gt;Dictionary&lt;/code&gt; checked with &lt;code&gt;TryGetValue&lt;/code&gt;) can still hide its users.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Reflection and dynamic loading&lt;/strong&gt; are only partly visible. Types a framework discovers this way run their whole test project.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;F# signature files&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;.fsi&lt;/code&gt;) are not analysed, so a change to one runs its project's dependents.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Tests skipped while recording&lt;/strong&gt; have no coverage, for example a missing Docker daemon. Record where your tests actually run.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The full list is on the &lt;a href="https://testdetta.com/docs/limitations/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Limitations page&lt;/a&gt;. My recommendation: keep running the full suite on your default branch. Recording does that anyway, so anything a selection could ever hide is still caught before release.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What's supported
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Test frameworks:&lt;/strong&gt; xUnit v2, xUnit v3, NUnit, MSTest and TUnit&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Runners:&lt;/strong&gt; VSTest and Microsoft.Testing.Platform (read from &lt;code&gt;global.json&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Languages:&lt;/strong&gt; C#, Visual Basic and F#&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;CI:&lt;/strong&gt; GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, Azure DevOps, Jenkins, Bitbucket and on-premises setups&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

