&lt;p&gt;How does your team deal with a slow .NET test suite today? I'm curious what has worked for you.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A single AI visibility score can hide the answer marketers most need: whether a brand appears when a prospective buyer asks a specific AI assistant for help. Fractl's research into generative engine optimization finds that brand recall differs substantially by model, category and prompt intent. For businesses trying to &lt;a href="https://scalevise.com/resources/ai-brand-mentions-citations-semrush-visibility-index/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;earn mentions in ChatGPT, Gemini or Claude&lt;/a&gt;, the practical implication is clear: visibility should be measured separately, not blended into one headline number.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fractl analyzed 8,090 keywords across 25 verticals and 22,410 leading domains. Its dataset covered 4,320 responses from GPT-4o in ChatGPT, Gemini 2.5 Flash and Claude Sonnet 4.6, using prompts intended to reflect buyer behavior in each category. The results, detailed in &lt;a href="https://www.frac.tl/generative-engine-optimization-brand-visibility-ai-search/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Fractl's Generative Engine Optimization study&lt;/a&gt;, show why a conventional ranking-style dashboard is an incomplete way to evaluate AI discovery.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Only &lt;strong&gt;11% of brands&lt;/strong&gt; in the dataset were referenced by all three models. About &lt;strong&gt;77% were mentioned by just one&lt;/strong&gt;. That means a brand can look visible in one assistant while being absent from another, even when users ask closely related questions. A blended score may smooth over that gap, but it cannot tell a marketing team where visibility is actually being won or lost.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why AI brand recall must be measured by model and intent
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The central finding is not that one model is universally better at naming brands. It is that each model can produce a different set of recommendations, and those differences change with the category and question. A travel prompt, an insurance question and a lifestyle recommendation do not create the same competitive field. Fractl observed concentrated travel results, a greater presence for digital-native insurance players, and direct-to-consumer brands appearing prominently in lifestyle-related results.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That makes category context as important as the model itself. A business that sells into more than one use case should not test one broad question and treat the result as representative. It should assess the buyer questions that matter to its own products, services and market position.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
  &lt;thead&gt;
    &lt;tr&gt;
      &lt;th&gt;Brand visibility pattern in Fractl's dataset&lt;/th&gt;
      &lt;th&gt;Share of brands&lt;/th&gt;
      &lt;th&gt;What it means for an audit&lt;/th&gt;
    &lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;/thead&gt;
  &lt;tbody&gt;
    &lt;tr&gt;
      &lt;td&gt;Referenced by GPT-4o, Gemini 2.5 Flash and Claude Sonnet 4.6&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;td&gt;11%&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;td&gt;Broad recall across the three tested models, but still dependent on the tested prompts and categories.&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;/tr&gt;
    &lt;tr&gt;
      &lt;td&gt;Referenced by only one of the three models&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;td&gt;About 77%&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;td&gt;A single-model result can overstate a brand's reach across AI assistants.&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fractl also identified a &lt;strong&gt;default brands effect&lt;/strong&gt;. Some brands surfaced repeatedly across prompts and categories, even when they were not the leaders in traditional SEO metrics. At the same time, conventional search authority generally correlated with AI recall for most brands. The relationship was not absolute: roughly 5% of brands were underexposed in large language models relative to their search standing, while about 4% were AI overperformers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For marketers, this is a useful distinction. Strong organic search performance remains relevant, but it is not a guarantee that an AI assistant will mention a brand. Nor does weak conventional visibility necessarily rule out strong AI recall in a particular category.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  A practical model-by-model audit
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A useful audit starts with &lt;a href="https://scalevise.com/resources/google-ai-mode-buyer-queries-seo-content-gaps/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;the questions buyers are likely to ask&lt;/a&gt;, rather than a generic prompt such as "best companies in [category]." Group prompts by category and intent. For example, separate research-oriented questions, comparison questions and recommendation requests where those reflect real customer journeys.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then document which brands each model names, how often they recur across the prompt set, and which sources the models cite where citations are provided. Fractl's GEO framework recommends looking at these cited sources because they can indicate the types of coverage associated with credibility in an AI-generated answer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A practical workflow should include:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Map model coverage&lt;/strong&gt; by recording whether ChatGPT, Gemini and Claude mention the brand for each relevant prompt.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Segment the prompt set&lt;/strong&gt; by product category and buyer intent, so results do not collapse unrelated questions into one score.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://scalevise.com/resources/ai-citations-brand-sentiment-study/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Review cited sources&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; to identify publications, pages or third-party references that appear alongside AI answers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Compare recurring competitors&lt;/strong&gt; to see which brands have become defaults for the category.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Repeat the audit over time&lt;/strong&gt;, because model versions, underlying data and prompt behavior can change results.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This approach does not turn AI recall into a fully controllable metric. It does make the gap between a brand's desired category position and its current AI presence visible enough to act on.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Improve category signals beyond owned content
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fractl argues that improving recall depends less on simply publishing more generic content and more on reinforcing clear category associations across the wider web. Owned pages remain part of that work, especially when they explain what a company offers in precise, useful language. But the study's framework places substantial weight on &lt;a href="https://scalevise.com/resources/ai-search-business-review-replies-third-party-coverage/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;third-party coverage and digital PR&lt;/a&gt; that support the same association through high-authority publishers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In practice, that means teams should connect content work with earned-media efforts. If a business wants to be associated with a defined product category, its website, expert commentary, research, partnerships and relevant external coverage should consistently communicate that positioning. The goal is not to chase mentions indiscriminately. It is to build credible, category-relevant signals that make the association easier to recognize across the web ecosystem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The study's scope also sets important limits. It tested three AI models and a fixed prompt set, so its percentages should not be treated as a permanent market-wide benchmark. Results may change as models, their underlying data and the ways people phrase queries evolve. The durable lesson is methodological: measure the models and questions that matter to the business, then avoid treating one aggregated score as the whole story.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For businesses that rely on search discovery, this shift affects everyday marketing workflows. SEO reporting can remain a useful baseline, but it should sit alongside a recurring AI visibility review that tracks category prompts, competitor recall and external sources. That provides a more specific basis for deciding whether to strengthen a product page, develop a research asset, pursue relevant coverage or refine the way a category is described.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Businesses that cannot see how AI assistants describe their category risk spending on content without knowing whether it changes discovery. GEO Search Leads helps teams turn model-by-model findings into a practical plan for stronger &lt;a href="https://geosearchleads.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AI search visibility and qualified lead opportunities&lt;/a&gt;, connecting category positioning, cited-source analysis and measurable follow-up work. Start an AI visibility audit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Frequently Asked Questions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What does AI visibility mean for a brand?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AI visibility is the extent to which an AI assistant mentions or recommends a brand in response to relevant user prompts. Fractl's research indicates that this visibility varies by model, category and prompt intent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why is one AI visibility score misleading?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fractl found that only 11% of brands in its dataset were referenced by all three tested models, while about 77% were mentioned by only one. A combined score can conceal major differences between assistants.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Which AI models did Fractl study?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The study analyzed responses from GPT-4o in ChatGPT, Gemini 2.5 Flash and Claude Sonnet 4.6.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How can a business improve its AI brand recall?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fractl recommends auditing relevant prompts by model and intent, reviewing cited sources, and strengthening category associations through owned content, third-party coverage and digital PR.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  Conclusion
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fractl's findings make the case for treating AI visibility as a set of model-specific, category-specific signals rather than a single marketing metric. Brands that audit the buyer questions relevant to their market, identify gaps across assistants and build credible category signals across the web will have a more useful foundation for AI search strategy than those relying on one blended score.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


  Automating Multi‑Platform Content Publishing: Fixing Bluesky JSON Validation and Keeping Metadata in Sync
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TL;DR:&lt;/strong&gt; I updated the content‑automation pipeline to generate a schema‑compliant Bluesky JSON payload and synchronized the &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; flags after each successful publish. The changes eliminate runtime validation errors and give us a reliable source‑of‑truth for what has been generated.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  The Problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our weekly “content‑automation” job creates markdown articles for Medium and Substack, then builds platform‑specific payloads for Bluesky. The last run (2026‑10‑14) failed with the following error from the Bluesky validation endpoint:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight http"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;POST https://api.bsky.app/xrpc/com.atproto.repo.createRecord
Response 400: {"error":"InvalidRequest","message":"JSON schema validation failed: required property 'text' missing"}
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The script was reading &lt;code&gt;content/2026/10/13/VS/bluesky_es.json&lt;/code&gt;, but the generated file was missing the required &lt;code&gt;text&lt;/code&gt; field and contained an extra &lt;code&gt;type&lt;/code&gt; value that the API rejects. At the same time, &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; still reported &lt;code&gt;"medium_generated": false&lt;/code&gt; even though the Medium markdown files had been written, causing our CI checks to think the publish step was incomplete.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  What I Tried First
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My first attempt was to patch the JSON after it was written, adding a &lt;code&gt;text&lt;/code&gt; property manually:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// scripts/generate_bluesky_payload.js (initial version)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;parse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;readFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;srcPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;utf8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// quick fix&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;destPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;I also added a post‑publish step that toggled the flags in &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// scripts/update_metadata.js (initial version)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;parse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;readFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;content/2026/10/14/content-automation/metadata.json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;medium_generated&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;metaPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Both patches compiled, but the API still rejected the payload because the &lt;code&gt;type&lt;/code&gt; field in the JSON array (&lt;code&gt;"avance"&lt;/code&gt; for Spanish, &lt;code&gt;"progress"&lt;/code&gt; for English) is not part of the allowed schema. Moreover, the manual &lt;code&gt;text&lt;/code&gt; injection left the content empty, resulting in blank posts on Bluesky.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  The Implementation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  1. Refactor the Bluesky payload generator
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The generator now builds the payload from a &lt;strong&gt;typed model&lt;/strong&gt; instead of copying raw JSON. I introduced &lt;code&gt;src/models/blueskyPost.js&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/models/blueskyPost.js&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;BlueskyPost&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="cm"&gt;/**
   * @param {string} language  'en' | 'es'
   * @param {string} headline  Title of the post
   * @param {string} body      Full markdown content
   */&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;constructor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;language&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;headline&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;$type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;app.bsky.feed.post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;language&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;headline&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;\n\n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;createdAt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toISOString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;toJSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Only keep properties required by the API&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;$type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;createdAt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;$type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;createdAt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nx"&gt;module&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;exports&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;BlueskyPost&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The generator script (&lt;code&gt;scripts/generate_bluesky_payload.js&lt;/code&gt;) now imports this model:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
