&lt;h2&gt;


  Understanding Port Errors (Diagnostic Table)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before executing commands, analyze the exact error message from your client (browser, terminal, or API). This helps narrow down the root cause.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Connection Refused&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
The TCP connection attempt reached a destination, but was actively rejected (e.g., via a TCP RST packet).&lt;br&gt;
Service is down, service bound to wrong IP, host firewall REJECT rule, or upstream device rejection.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Operation Timed Out&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
The connection attempt received no response within the expected timeframe.&lt;br&gt;
Firewall DROP rule, routing issue, packet loss, or the remote server/interface is unresponsive.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 1: Verify the Application Status
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A port cannot accept connections if the underlying application (e.g., Nginx, SSH, MySQL) is stopped or crashed. Check the systemd service status:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;systemctl status &amp;lt;service_name&amp;gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Look for Active: &lt;code&gt;active (running)&lt;/code&gt;. However, an active status does not guarantee the service is listening on the expected port. If the service shows as failed, investigate the recent logs to find the exact crash reason before proceeding:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;journalctl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-u&lt;/span&gt; &amp;lt;service_name&amp;gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-n&lt;/span&gt; 100 &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--no-pager&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 2: Check Socket States and IP&amp;nbsp;Binding
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your application might be running but configured to accept only local traffic. Use the ss utility (the modern, preferred alternative to netstat) to inspect listening sockets.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;ss &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-tulnp&lt;/span&gt; | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;':&amp;lt;port_number&amp;gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How to interpret the local address output:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;127.0.0.1:&amp;lt;port&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;[::1]:&amp;lt;port&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;: The service is bound to localhost. It will only accept connections from inside the server itself. Note: This is the correct and secure configuration for backend services like databases meant to be accessed only via a local reverse proxy.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;0.0.0.0:&amp;lt;port&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;*:&amp;lt;port&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;: The service is bound to all available IPv4 interfaces.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;Public_IP&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;port&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;: The service is explicitly bound to a specific network interface.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If external access is required but the service is bound to 127.0.0.1, you must update the application's configuration file to bind to the appropriate public-facing interface or 0.0.0.0.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 3: Inspect OS-Level Firewalls
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the service is running and properly bound, the host operating system's firewall may be blocking the connection. Ensure you only allow the specific protocol (TCP or UDP) your application requires.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;For Ubuntu / Debian (UFW):&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
First, check the current active rules:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;ufw status verbose
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If the required rule is missing, add it (e.g., for TCP port 80):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;ufw allow 80/tcp
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;For RHEL / AlmaLinux / Rocky Linux (Firewalld):&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Check the active rules for your default zone:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;firewall-cmd &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--list-all&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If the port is missing, add it permanently and reload the runtime configuration. (Note: If your public interface is assigned to a specific zone, append --zone=public to the command).&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;firewall-cmd &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--add-port&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;80/tcp &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--permanent&lt;/span&gt; 
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;firewall-cmd &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--reload&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 4: Docker Port Publishing (If Applicable)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If your application runs inside a Docker container, you must publish the internal container port to the host machine. Inspect the specific container's mappings:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;docker port &amp;lt;container_name_or_id&amp;gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Security Warning&lt;/strong&gt;: When you publish a port using &lt;code&gt;-p 8080:80&lt;/code&gt;, Docker binds to &lt;code&gt;0.0.0.0&lt;/code&gt; by default and actively modifies iptables, often bypassing UFW rules. If the service should not be publicly accessible, bind it specifically to localhost (&lt;code&gt;-p 127.0.0.1:8080:80&lt;/code&gt;) or manage it through a dedicated Docker network.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 5: Verify Upstream Network Filtering
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the host firewall is configured correctly, the restriction may exist outside your OS. As a dedicated server user, check your provider's control panel.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Locate Security Groups, Hardware Firewalls, or Network ACLs in your client dashboard.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Verify that an inbound rule exists allowing the correct destination port and specific protocol (TCP or UDP).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If no provider firewall is active, the issue could involve upstream routing, missing public IP configurations, or an ISP block.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 6: Advanced Diagnostics with&amp;nbsp;TCPDump
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To definitively prove where a connection is failing, use tcpdump to capture network traffic arriving at your server.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Run this command on your server (monitoring all interfaces for a specific TCP port):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;tcpdump &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-ni&lt;/span&gt; any &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'tcp port &amp;lt;port_number&amp;gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;While this runs, attempt to connect to the port from your local workstation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How to read the capture:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Incoming SYN packets are visible: The TCP connection attempt is reaching your server. The issue lies within the server itself (check listening sockets, host firewall drops, and outbound routing for the response).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;SYN followed by a RST packet: The connection is reaching the server but being actively rejected. Re-verify the IP/port binding and check for firewall REJECT rules.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;SYN arrives, but no response is sent: The packet reached the server, but the system dropped it. Check the host firewall DROP rules or kernel routing behavior.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No packets are captured: The filter may be incorrect, the client may be querying the wrong IP/IPv6 address, or the traffic is being blocked upstream (provider network firewall, routing failure, or client-side ISP).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  FAQ
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why is my port 80 or 443&amp;nbsp;blocked?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
If you cannot access your web server, you must verify the web server's listening state, ensure the host firewall allows traffic, and check your provider's network filtering policies. For HTTPS (443), you must also independently verify your TLS/SSL configuration.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Is netstat deprecated in&amp;nbsp;Linux?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
While netstat (part of net-tools) may still be available on some systems, ss is generally preferred for modern Linux socket troubleshooting. It provides faster execution, deeper TCP state information, and is actively maintained.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Does a reboot fix a blocked&amp;nbsp;port?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Generally, no. A reboot may resolve transient crashes or stuck processes, but it will not fix misconfigured bind addresses, missing firewall rules, or incorrect Docker port mappings. Always check system logs and network states before relying on a reboot.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It was a simple question. Why did we choose that queue over the other one? You knew the answer. You had argued for it in a design discussion two months ago. And yet, with twelve faces on the call looking at you, nothing came. A second of silence, then three more. You said something vague, the meeting moved on, and you replayed it for the rest of the day.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This happens to almost everyone, including people who seem endlessly fluent. Under pressure, recall gets harder. It is not a sign that you do not know your work. It is a sign that you are a person being watched.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The useful thing is not to stop it from ever happening. It is to have a plan for when it does.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Give yourself permission to pause on purpose. Good question, let me think for a second. Said calmly, a pause like that sounds like care. Most people in the meeting will barely notice it, and the second you buy is often enough for the answer to arrive.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with what you do remember, even if it is only part of it. We compared both on cost and on how they handle retries, and I can walk through the rest. Starting to speak often brings the rest back with it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If nothing comes, say so honestly and promise something specific. I do not want to give you half an answer. I will send the reasoning in the channel this afternoon. Then do it, well, before the end of the day. A clear written answer a few hours later often lands better than a perfect spoken one would have.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prepare for the questions you can predict. Before a meeting where your work is discussed, write down the three questions you would least like to be asked and one line for each. You rarely need the notes. Having written them is what helps.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And go easy on yourself afterwards. The meeting has forgotten that silence already. Only you are still replaying it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;– Asael Shinder&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In April we sold for four days at last month's prices. Our largest supplier had raised theirs on the first of the month and sent the new price files as usual. They had arrived. They had just never been loaded.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The supplier uploads over SFTP into an S3 bucket. Each new object triggers an event notification, which invokes a Lambda that reads the object key from the event, fetches the file and loads the prices. That week the supplier upgraded their system, and the files changed from PRICES_20260401.csv to Price List 2026-04-01.csv.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The object key in an S3 event notification is URL encoded, and the AWS documentation says so with exactly this example: a space arrives as a plus sign. Our Lambda took the key as it came and asked for Price+List+2026-04-01.csv, which did not exist. It did not get a 404. Its role had s3:GetObject on the bucket but not s3:ListBucket, and without list permission S3 answers a request for a missing key with 403 Access Denied, so that a caller cannot find out which keys exist. We spent the first day reading IAM policies, the bucket policy and the key policy, all of which were fine.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The rest of the delay was ours too. S3 invokes Lambda asynchronously, and a failed asynchronous invocation is retried twice and then dropped unless the function has somewhere to send failures. Ours had nowhere. The files sat in the bucket and the events that announced them were gone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The handler now decodes the key with unquote_plus before using it, and its tests use file names with spaces, plus signs, ampersands and accented letters. The role has ListBucket on the inbound prefix, so a missing object is reported as missing. Failed invocations go to an SQS queue with an alert on its depth. And an hourly job lists the inbound prefix, compares it with the files we have loaded, and alerts on anything older than thirty minutes that we have not.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An event about a file is a description of it, written in someone else's encoding. Ours described the file correctly, and we read it as if it were the name.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;– Sergey Shinder&lt;/p&gt;
